  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q16-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Line $A$ has equation $2x+ky=5$.\n\nLine $B$ passes through the points $(1,5)$ and $(4+k,6)$.\n\nFind the value of $k$ if the lines $A$ and $B$ do not intersect.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=-2$",
      "worked": "The lines must be parallel and distinct.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}m_A&=-\\frac{2}{k}\\\\m_B&=\\frac{6-5}{4+k-1}\\\\&=\\frac{1}{3+k}\\end{align*}$$\n\nEquating gradients:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-\\frac{2}{k}&=\\frac{1}{3+k}\\\\-2(3+k)&=k\\\\-6-2k&=k\\\\k&=-2\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $k=-2$, line $A$ is $y=x-\\frac{5}{2}$ and line $B$ is $y=x+4$. Their gradients are equal and their intercepts differ, so they do not intersect.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
